    "summary": "High‑energy El Paso show with monster He’s Gone → Truckin' → The Other One",
    "blurb": "Features a blazing He’s Gone → Truckin’ → The Other One suite and a soaring Eyes of the World. Jerry’s guitar is on fire while Phil’s bass drives the band, with solid keyboards and tight drums throughout.",
    "review": "The November 23, 1973 County Coliseum gig captures the Dead in peak form during their Texas thunder stretch. From the opening set the band rolls through a breezy mix of country‑rockers and folk‑blues—Bertha, Mexicali Blues, Deal—and then builds momentum with Jack Straw, China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider, El Paso and a tightly executed Weather Report Suite that segues into Let It Grow. The second set is where the night truly shines: a monster He’s Gone → Truckin’ → The Other One jam erupts with blistering guitar lines and a bebop‑like drum drive, followed by a luminous Eyes of the World and a rousing Casey Jones to close. Throughout the evening Jerry delivers intense shredding and soaring tone, Phil’s bass propels every groove, Keith weaves bright textures into the improvisations, and Bill Kreutzmann holds down a crisp, driving pulse. The pristine soundboard source captures every nuance, making this performance one of the most compelling late‑1973 documents.",
